 1 big thing: A trillion-dollar rethink 
 
 By Pete Gannon 
 &nbsp; 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 Illustration: Lindsey Bailey/Axios

 
 
 
 &nbsp; 
 
 Just as public markets are growing increasingly skeptical over the AI trade, private investors are reportedly reconsidering Anthropic's mind-blowingly high $2 trillion valuation.

 Why it matters: What a year this week has been.

 🕸️ The big picture: Anthropic sits at the economic center of the AI boom, and its coming IPO could ripple through the AI trade at a time of many ripples.

 Its nearly $900 billion in compute and infrastructure commitments ties its fortunes to companies across the AI economy, from chip manufacturers and networking companies to data center operators and power suppliers. ‼️ Stunning stat: Anthropic's valuation being discussed today is about $1 trillion lower than just a month ago.

 It entered October targeting $2 trillion . After AI stocks plummeted yesterday, and investors considered the possible effects of Tuesday's midterms, discussions of Anthropic's valuation had fallen as low as $1 trillion, WSJ reported . 🔄 The intrigue: The IPO — if it happens later in November — would have an equally sizable impact on public markets.

 Max Kettner, chief multi-asset strategist at HSBC, described the current AI trade in an interview with Bloomberg Radio as driven by a series of narratives. And right now that narrative — which he doesn't believe — is that the AI cycle is dead. Anthropic's IPO is a key catalyst to change it — but that could mean pricing the IPO at a lower valuation. The worst-case scenario for an already shaky AI trade is a repeat of SpaceX's IPO, Kettner said — where initial buying euphoria quickly reversed, and the stock stayed mired under its offering price for over a month. The bottom line: Anthropic's IPO could help the battered AI trade regain its footing — or provide another reason for investors to run for the exits.

