 4. 🕔 After hours: A stainless-steel flop 
 
 
 &nbsp; 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 Photo illustration: Aïda Amer/Axios. Photo: Gary Hershorn/Getty Images

 
 
 
 &nbsp; 
 
 Cybertruck, we hardly knew you.

 Elon Musk says Tesla will wind down production next year to make room for its autonomous Cybercab, Axios Future of Mobility author Joann Muller writes . Musk once envisioned selling at least 250,000 Cybertrucks a year. Sales peaked below 40,000 in 2024 and have been falling ever since. Flashback: Tesla's futuristic truck got off to a rocky start in 2019 when its supposedly unbreakable windows shattered during a demonstration.

 What went wrong: Owners complained of rust spots, stuck accelerator pedals and malfunctioning windshield wipers. Some even found their rugged trucks stranded in snow or sand.

 Tesla recalled the Cybertruck 11 times, including twice to reattach stainless steel trim pieces that could fly off while driving. Neither the truck's $39,900 price target nor its promised 500-mile driving range ever materialized. Most buyers paid closer to $100,000. The irony: Automotive journalists generally admired its engineering.

 The bottom line: The stainless-steel, sharp-edged behemoth attracted plenty of attention, but not enough buyers.

 
 
